IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `oas-core`
IMPORT `oas-core-measures`

-- Old Age Security Act, Part IV, "Oaths and Affidavits" (s 38), "Agreements" (ss 39 to 43), "Consolidated Revenue Fund" (s 45) and
-- "Administration and Annual Report" (ss 46 and 47).
-- Section 40(2) (the Governor in Council's regulations to give effect to an agreement) and ss 42(3) to (6) (the Houses' procedure on a motion to
-- revoke an order) are inert; see NOTES.md, "Coverage table".
-- Hohfeld: the Minister's power to make an agreement (39, 40) and the Governor in Council's power to declare it in force (41) are liabilities of the
-- province, the foreign government and, where the agreement reaches them, of the person; the Minister's duty to report (47) is Parliament's claim.
-- No person's right is created by these sections, so the "Jural relations" table in NOTES.md lists them as powers and duties between officials.

§ `Oaths and Affidavits`

-- s 38(1): an officer or employee of Her Majesty, authorized by the Minister, has "all the powers of a commissioner for taking affidavits" for the
-- oath, affidavit, declaration or affirmation, in the course of their employment and subject to any other Act of Parliament or of a legislature.
-- src:1010 | 38 (1) Any officer or employee of Her Majesty who is authorized by the Minister for the purpose may, in the course of their employment and subject to any other Act of Parliament or any Act of the legislature of a province, administer oaths and take and receive affidavits, declarations and solemn affirmations and every person so authorized has, with respect to any such oath, affidavit, declaration or affirmation, all the powers of a commissioner for taking affidavits.
@ref R.S.C., 1985, c. O-9, s 38(1) (src:1010)
GIVEN `the person is an officer or employee of Her Majesty who is authorized by the Minister for the purpose` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the oath is administered, or the affidavit, declaration or solemn affirmation is taken and received, in the course of the person's employment` IS A BOOLEAN
      `another Act of Parliament or an Act of the legislature of a province provides otherwise` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 38(1): the person has all the powers of a commissioner for taking affidavits with respect to the oath, affidavit, declaration or affirmation` `the person is an officer or employee of Her Majesty who is authorized by the Minister for the purpose` `the oath is administered, or the affidavit, declaration or solemn affirmation is taken and received, in the course of the person's employment` `another Act of Parliament or an Act of the legislature of a province provides otherwise` MEANS
        `the person is an officer or employee of Her Majesty who is authorized by the Minister for the purpose`
    AND `the oath is administered, or the affidavit, declaration or solemn affirmation is taken and received, in the course of the person's employment`
    AND (NOT `another Act of Parliament or an Act of the legislature of a province provides otherwise`)

-- (2): the Minister may accept an oath, affidavit, declaration or solemn affirmation of an officer or employee of a federal or provincial department
-- who has all the powers of a commissioner for taking affidavits.
-- src:1012 | (2) The Minister may accept, for the purposes of the administration of this Act or the regulations, any oath administered or affidavit, declaration or solemn affirmation given by any officer or employee of any department in, or other portion of, the federal public administration specified in Schedule I, IV or V to the Financial Administration Act or of any department of the government of a province who has all the powers of a commissioner for taking affidavits.
@ref R.S.C., 1985, c. O-9, s 38(2) (src:1012)
GIVEN `the oath, affidavit, declaration or solemn affirmation is given by an officer or employee of a department in, or other portion of, the federal public administration specified in Schedule I, IV or V to the Financial Administration Act, or of a department of the government of a province` IS A BOOLEAN
      `that officer or employee has all the powers of a commissioner for taking affidavits` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it is accepted for the purposes of the administration of this Act or the regulations` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 38(2): the Minister may accept the oath, affidavit, declaration or solemn affirmation` `the oath, affidavit, declaration or solemn affirmation is given by an officer or employee of a department in, or other portion of, the federal public administration specified in Schedule I, IV or V to the Financial Administration Act, or of a department of the government of a province` `that officer or employee has all the powers of a commissioner for taking affidavits` `it is accepted for the purposes of the administration of this Act or the regulations` MEANS
        `the oath, affidavit, declaration or solemn affirmation is given by an officer or employee of a department in, or other portion of, the federal public administration specified in Schedule I, IV or V to the Financial Administration Act, or of a department of the government of a province`
    AND `that officer or employee has all the powers of a commissioner for taking affidavits`
    AND `it is accepted for the purposes of the administration of this Act or the regulations`

§ `Agreements`

-- s 39(1): payment of a provincial benefit with the benefit under this Act, by agreement with the province, with the approval of the Governor in Council.
-- src:1016 | 39 (1) Where a province provides benefits similar to or as a supplement to benefits payable under this Act for a pensioner or a pensioner’s spouse or common-law partner within that province, the Minister may, with the approval of the Governor in Council, enter into an agreement with the government of that province whereby the provincial benefit that is payable to a pensioner or a pensioner’s spouse or common-law partner may be included with the amount of the benefit under this Act and paid on behalf of the government of that province in such manner as the agreement may provide.
@ref R.S.C., 1985, c. O-9, s 39(1) (src:1016)
GIVEN `the province provides benefits similar to or as a supplement to benefits payable under this Act for a pensioner or a pensioner's spouse or common-law partner within that province` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the Governor in Council approves the agreement` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 39(1): the Minister may enter into an agreement with the government of the province` `the province provides benefits similar to or as a supplement to benefits payable under this Act for a pensioner or a pensioner's spouse or common-law partner within that province` `the Governor in Council approves the agreement` MEANS
        `the province provides benefits similar to or as a supplement to benefits payable under this Act for a pensioner or a pensioner's spouse or common-law partner within that province`
    AND `the Governor in Council approves the agreement`

-- (1.1): the agreement may provide for the Minister to administer the provincial benefits on the terms and conditions the agreement sets out.
-- src:1018 | (1.1) The agreement may provide for the Minister to administer the provincial benefits on behalf of the government of that province in accordance with the terms and conditions set out in the agreement.
@ref R.S.C., 1985, c. O-9, s 39(1.1) (src:1018)
GIVEN `the agreement provides for the Minister to administer the provincial benefits on behalf of the government of that province` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the Minister administers them in accordance with the terms and conditions set out in the agreement` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 39(1.1): the Minister may administer the provincial benefits on behalf of the government of the province` `the agreement provides for the Minister to administer the provincial benefits on behalf of the government of that province` `the Minister administers them in accordance with the terms and conditions set out in the agreement` MEANS
        `the agreement provides for the Minister to administer the provincial benefits on behalf of the government of that province`
    AND `the Minister administers them in accordance with the terms and conditions set out in the agreement`

-- (2): "It shall be a term of the agreement that the government of the province ... shall reimburse the Minister".
-- src:1020 | (2) It shall be a term of the agreement that the government of the province that is a party to the agreement shall reimburse the Minister for the expenses incurred by the Minister under that agreement.
@ref R.S.C., 1985, c. O-9, s 39(2) (src:1020)
GIVEN `the agreement has a term that the government of the province shall reimburse the Minister for the expenses incurred by the Minister under that agreement` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 39(2): the agreement has the term the Act requires` `the agreement has a term that the government of the province shall reimburse the Minister for the expenses incurred by the Minister under that agreement` MEANS
    `the agreement has a term that the government of the province shall reimburse the Minister for the expenses incurred by the Minister under that agreement`

-- s 40(1): reciprocal arrangements with another country. The five matters of paragraphs (a) to (e) are listed "without restricting the generality".
-- src:1023 | (a) the exchange of such information obtained under that law or this Act as may be necessary to give effect to any such arrangements;
-- src:1024 | (b) the administration of benefits payable under this Act to persons resident in that country, the extension of benefits under that law or this Act to persons employed in or resident in that country and the increase or decrease in the amount of the benefits payable under that law or this Act to persons employed in or resident in that country;
-- src:1025 | (c) the administration of benefits payable under that law to persons resident in Canada, the extension of benefits under that law or this Act to persons employed in or resident in Canada and the increase or decrease in the amount of the benefits payable under that law or this Act to persons employed in or resident in Canada;
-- src:1026 | (d) the totalization of periods of residence and periods of contribution in that country and periods of residence in Canada; and
-- src:1027 | (e) the payment by that country and Canada respectively, where applicable as a result of totalization, of prorated benefits based on periods of residence and periods of contribution in that country and periods of residence in Canada.
@ref R.S.C., 1985, c. O-9, s 40(1) (src:1023-1027)
DECLARE `s 40(1): Matter of a reciprocal arrangement` IS ONE OF
    `s 40(1)(a): the exchange of information`
    `s 40(1)(b): the administration and extension of benefits abroad`
    `s 40(1)(c): the administration and extension of benefits in Canada`
    `s 40(1)(d): the totalization of periods of residence and contribution`
    `s 40(1)(e): the payment of prorated benefits`

-- src:1022 | 40 (1) Where, under any law of a country other than Canada, provision is made for the payment of old age or other benefits including survivors’ or disability benefits, the Minister may, on behalf of the Government of Canada, on such terms and conditions as may be approved by the Governor in Council, enter into an agreement with the government of that country for the making of reciprocal arrangements relating to the administration or operation of that law and of this Act, including, without restricting the generality of the foregoing, arrangements relating to
@ref R.S.C., 1985, c. O-9, s 40(1) (src:1022)
GIVEN `under a law of a country other than Canada provision is made for the payment of old age or other benefits, including survivors' or disability benefits` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the terms and conditions are approved by the Governor in Council` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 40(1): the Minister may enter into an agreement with the government of the country` `under a law of a country other than Canada provision is made for the payment of old age or other benefits, including survivors' or disability benefits` `the terms and conditions are approved by the Governor in Council` MEANS
        `under a law of a country other than Canada provision is made for the payment of old age or other benefits, including survivors' or disability benefits`
    AND `the terms and conditions are approved by the Governor in Council`

-- NOTHING is an arrangement that is not one of the five listed matters; it is still covered when it relates to the administration or operation of that law and of this Act.
-- "without restricting the generality": the five matters are examples (fork OA1-F23 of NOTES.md).
-- src:1022 | 40 (1) Where, under any law of a country other than Canada, provision is made for the payment of old age or other benefits including survivors’ or disability benefits, the Minister may, on behalf of the Government of Canada, on such terms and conditions as may be approved by the Governor in Council, enter into an agreement with the government of that country for the making of reciprocal arrangements relating to the administration or operation of that law and of this Act, including, without restricting the generality of the foregoing, arrangements relating to
-- src:1023 | (a) the exchange of such information obtained under that law or this Act as may be necessary to give effect to any such arrangements;
-- src:1024 | (b) the administration of benefits payable under this Act to persons resident in that country, the extension of benefits under that law or this Act to persons employed in or resident in that country and the increase or decrease in the amount of the benefits payable under that law or this Act to persons employed in or resident in that country;
-- src:1025 | (c) the administration of benefits payable under that law to persons resident in Canada, the extension of benefits under that law or this Act to persons employed in or resident in Canada and the increase or decrease in the amount of the benefits payable under that law or this Act to persons employed in or resident in Canada;
-- src:1026 | (d) the totalization of periods of residence and periods of contribution in that country and periods of residence in Canada; and
-- src:1027 | (e) the payment by that country and Canada respectively, where applicable as a result of totalization, of prorated benefits based on periods of residence and periods of contribution in that country and periods of residence in Canada.
@ref R.S.C., 1985, c. O-9, s 40(1) (src:1022-1027)
GIVEN `the matter of the arrangement` IS A MAYBE `s 40(1): Matter of a reciprocal arrangement`
      `the arrangement relates to the administration or operation of that law and of this Act` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 40(1): the agreement may make reciprocal arrangements on the matter` `the matter of the arrangement` `the arrangement relates to the administration or operation of that law and of this Act` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the matter of the arrangement`
    WHEN JUST listed THEN TRUE
    WHEN NOTHING THEN `the arrangement relates to the administration or operation of that law and of this Act`

-- s 41(1): the agreement "has the force of law in Canada during such period as by the terms of the agreement it remains in force" once the order comes into force.
-- src:1031 | 41 (1) The Governor in Council may, by order, declare any agreement entered into under section 40 to be in force and, when any such order comes into force, the agreement to which it relates has the force of law in Canada during such period as by the terms of the agreement it remains in force.
@ref R.S.C., 1985, c. O-9, s 41(1) (src:1031)
GIVEN `the day on which the order declaring the agreement to be in force comes into force` IS A DATE
      `the day on which the agreement ceases to be in force by its terms` IS A MAYBE DATE
      `the day` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 41(1): the agreement has the force of law in Canada on the day` `the day on which the order declaring the agreement to be in force comes into force` `the day on which the agreement ceases to be in force by its terms` `the day` MEANS
        `the day` AT LEAST `the day on which the order declaring the agreement to be in force comes into force`
    AND (CONSIDER `the day on which the agreement ceases to be in force by its terms`
         WHEN NOTHING THEN TRUE
         WHEN JUST ceased THEN `the day` LESS THAN ceased)

-- (2): notice of the day the agreement comes into force and of the day it ceases to be in force by proclamation published with the agreement's text in the Canada Gazette.
-- src:1033 | (2) Notice of the day an agreement entered into under section 40 comes into force and of the day it ceases to be in force shall be given by proclamation of the Governor in Council published, with the text of the agreement, in the Canada Gazette.
@ref R.S.C., 1985, c. O-9, s 41(2) (src:1033)
GIVEN `a proclamation of the Governor in Council giving notice of the day the agreement comes into force and of the day it ceases to be in force is published, with the text of the agreement, in the Canada Gazette` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 41(2): the notice has been given as the Act requires` `a proclamation of the Governor in Council giving notice of the day the agreement comes into force and of the day it ceases to be in force is published, with the text of the agreement, in the Canada Gazette` MEANS
    `a proclamation of the Governor in Council giving notice of the day the agreement comes into force and of the day it ceases to be in force is published, with the text of the agreement, in the Canada Gazette`

-- s 42(1): "within the first 15 days on which that House is sitting after the order is made".
-- src:1035 | 42 (1) An order under section 41 shall be laid before each House of Parliament within the first 15 days on which that House is sitting after the order is made.
@ref R.S.C., 1985, c. O-9, s 42(1) (src:1035)
GIVEN `the number of the sitting day of the House, counted from the first sitting day after the order is made, on which the order is laid before the House` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 42(1): the order has been laid before the House in time` `the number of the sitting day of the House, counted from the first sitting day after the order is made, on which the order is laid before the House` MEANS
        `the number of the sitting day of the House, counted from the first sitting day after the order is made, on which the order is laid before the House` AT LEAST 1
    AND `the number of the sitting day of the House, counted from the first sitting day after the order is made, on which the order is laid before the House` AT MOST 15

-- (2): comes into force on the thirtieth sitting day after it is laid unless, before the twentieth sitting day, a motion to revoke is filed with the Speaker signed
-- by not less than fifty members of the House of Commons (for a motion for that House) or not less than twenty members of the Senate (for that House).
-- src:1037 | (2) An order referred to in subsection (1) shall come into force on the thirtieth sitting day after it has been laid before Parliament pursuant to that subsection unless before the twentieth sitting day after the order has been laid before Parliament a motion for the consideration of either House, to the effect that the order be revoked, signed by not less than fifty members of the House of Commons in the case of a motion for the consideration of that House and by not less than twenty members of the Senate in the case of a motion for the consideration of the Senate, is filed with the Speaker of the appropriate House.
@ref R.S.C., 1985, c. O-9, s 42(2) (src:1037)
GIVEN `the greatest number of members of the House of Commons who signed a motion for consideration, to the effect that the order be revoked, filed with the Speaker before the twentieth sitting day after the order was laid` IS A NUMBER
      `the greatest number of members of the Senate who signed a motion for consideration, to the effect that the order be revoked, filed with the Speaker before the twentieth sitting day after the order was laid` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 42(2): the order comes into force on the thirtieth sitting day after it has been laid before Parliament` `the greatest number of members of the House of Commons who signed a motion for consideration, to the effect that the order be revoked, filed with the Speaker before the twentieth sitting day after the order was laid` `the greatest number of members of the Senate who signed a motion for consideration, to the effect that the order be revoked, filed with the Speaker before the twentieth sitting day after the order was laid` MEANS
    NOT (    `the greatest number of members of the House of Commons who signed a motion for consideration, to the effect that the order be revoked, filed with the Speaker before the twentieth sitting day after the order was laid` AT LEAST 50
         OR  `the greatest number of members of the Senate who signed a motion for consideration, to the effect that the order be revoked, filed with the Speaker before the twentieth sitting day after the order was laid` AT LEAST 20)

-- (7): "adopted by the House in which it was introduced and is concurred in by the other House": the order stands revoked.
-- src:1047 | (7) Where a motion taken up and considered in accordance with this section is adopted by the House in which it was introduced and is concurred in by the other House, the particular order to which the motion relates shall stand revoked but without prejudice to the making of a further order of a like nature to implement a subsequent agreement between the Government of Canada and the government of the country that was a party to the agreement to which the order related.
@ref R.S.C., 1985, c. O-9, s 42(7) (src:1047)
GIVEN `the motion is adopted by the House in which it was introduced` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the motion is concurred in by the other House` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 42(7): the order stands revoked` `the motion is adopted by the House in which it was introduced` `the motion is concurred in by the other House` MEANS
        `the motion is adopted by the House in which it was introduced`
    AND `the motion is concurred in by the other House`

-- (8): not adopted by the House in which it was introduced, or adopted but not concurred in by the other: the order comes into force immediately on the failure.
-- src:1049 | (8) Where a motion taken up and considered in accordance with this section is not adopted by the House in which it was introduced or is adopted, with or without amendments, by that House but is not concurred in by the other House, the particular order to which the motion relates comes into force immediately on the failure to adopt the motion or concur therein, as the case may be.
@ref R.S.C., 1985, c. O-9, s 42(8) (src:1049)
GIVEN `the motion is adopted by the House in which it was introduced` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the motion is concurred in by the other House` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 42(8): the order comes into force immediately on the failure to adopt the motion or concur in it` `the motion is adopted by the House in which it was introduced` `the motion is concurred in by the other House` MEANS
        (NOT `the motion is adopted by the House in which it was introduced`)
    OR  (`the motion is adopted by the House in which it was introduced` AND (NOT `the motion is concurred in by the other House`))

-- (9): "a day on which either House of Parliament sits shall be deemed to be a sitting day" for the purposes of subsection (2).
-- src:1051 | (9) For the purposes of subsection (2), a day on which either House of Parliament sits shall be deemed to be a sitting day.
@ref R.S.C., 1985, c. O-9, s 42(9) (src:1051)
GIVEN `the House of Commons sits on the day` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the Senate sits on the day` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 42(9): the day is a sitting day for the purposes of subsection 42(2)` `the House of Commons sits on the day` `the Senate sits on the day` MEANS
        `the House of Commons sits on the day`
    OR  `the Senate sits on the day`

-- s 43: when each House has enacted rules for negative resolution, section 42 is repealed and a later order is an order subject to negative resolution.
-- src:1053 | 43 When each House of Parliament enacts rules whereby any regulation made subject to negative resolution of Parliament within the meaning of section 39 of the Interpretation Act may be made the subject of a resolution of both Houses of Parliament introduced and passed in accordance with the rules of those Houses, section 42 of this Act is thereupon repealed and an order made thereafter under section 41 is an order made subject to negative resolution of Parliament within the meaning of section 39 of the Interpretation Act.
@ref R.S.C., 1985, c. O-9, s 43 (src:1053)
GIVEN `each House of Parliament enacts rules whereby any regulation made subject to negative resolution of Parliament within the meaning of section 39 of the Interpretation Act may be made the subject of a resolution of both Houses introduced and passed in accordance with the rules of those Houses` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 43: section 42 is thereupon repealed` `each House of Parliament enacts rules whereby any regulation made subject to negative resolution of Parliament within the meaning of section 39 of the Interpretation Act may be made the subject of a resolution of both Houses introduced and passed in accordance with the rules of those Houses` MEANS
    `each House of Parliament enacts rules whereby any regulation made subject to negative resolution of Parliament within the meaning of section 39 of the Interpretation Act may be made the subject of a resolution of both Houses introduced and passed in accordance with the rules of those Houses`

-- src:1053 | 43 When each House of Parliament enacts rules whereby any regulation made subject to negative resolution of Parliament within the meaning of section 39 of the Interpretation Act may be made the subject of a resolution of both Houses of Parliament introduced and passed in accordance with the rules of those Houses, section 42 of this Act is thereupon repealed and an order made thereafter under section 41 is an order made subject to negative resolution of Parliament within the meaning of section 39 of the Interpretation Act.
@ref R.S.C., 1985, c. O-9, s 43 (src:1053)
GIVEN `section 42 has been repealed under section 43` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the order is made under section 41 after that repeal` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 43: the order is an order made subject to negative resolution of Parliament within the meaning of section 39 of the Interpretation Act` `section 42 has been repealed under section 43` `the order is made under section 41 after that repeal` MEANS
        `section 42 has been repealed under section 43`
    AND `the order is made under section 41 after that repeal`

§ `Consolidated Revenue Fund`

-- src:1137 | 45 All benefits payable under this Act shall be paid out of the Consolidated Revenue Fund.
@ref R.S.C., 1985, c. O-9, s 45 (src:1137)
`s 45: the fund out of which all benefits payable under this Act shall be paid` MEANS "the Consolidated Revenue Fund"

§ `Administration and Annual Report`

-- src:1141 | 46 This Act shall be administered by the Minister of Employment and Social Development.
@ref R.S.C., 1985, c. O-9, s 46 (src:1141)
`s 46: the one who administers this Act` MEANS Minister

-- s 47: the Minister reports annually, as soon as possible after the termination of each fiscal year if Parliament is then in session, or if not, as soon as possible
-- after the commencement of the next session. The Act fixes no last day; the rule gives the day from which the Minister is to report as soon as possible.
-- src:1144 | 47 The Minister shall submit to Parliament annually, as soon as possible after the termination of each fiscal year, if Parliament is then in session or, if not, as soon as possible after the commencement of the next session of Parliament, a report covering the administration of this Act and including an account of receipts and disbursements during the previous fiscal year.
@ref R.S.C., 1985, c. O-9, s 47 (src:1144)
GIVEN `the day on which the fiscal year terminates` IS A DATE
      `Parliament is in session on that day` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the day on which the next session of Parliament commences` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`s 47: the day from which the Minister is to submit the report as soon as possible` `the day on which the fiscal year terminates` `Parliament is in session on that day` `the day on which the next session of Parliament commences` MEANS
    IF `Parliament is in session on that day`
    THEN `the day on which the fiscal year terminates`
    ELSE `the day on which the next session of Parliament commences`

-- src:1144 | 47 The Minister shall submit to Parliament annually, as soon as possible after the termination of each fiscal year, if Parliament is then in session or, if not, as soon as possible after the commencement of the next session of Parliament, a report covering the administration of this Act and including an account of receipts and disbursements during the previous fiscal year.
@ref R.S.C., 1985, c. O-9, s 47 (src:1144)
GIVEN `the report covers the administration of this Act` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the report includes an account of receipts and disbursements during the previous fiscal year` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 47: the report is what the Act requires` `the report covers the administration of this Act` `the report includes an account of receipts and disbursements during the previous fiscal year` MEANS
        `the report covers the administration of this Act`
    AND `the report includes an account of receipts and disbursements during the previous fiscal year`
